rning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 23 Sep 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Single-stream Policy Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Zhongwen Xu , cite7†Zihan Ding L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Single-stream Policy Optimization, by Zhongwen Xu and Zihan Ding\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We revisit policy-gradient optimization for Large Language Models (LLMs) from a single-stream perspective. Prevailing group-based methods like GRPO reduce variance with on-the-fly baselines but suffer from critical flaws: frequent degenerate groups erase learning signals, and synchronization barriers hinder scalability. We introduce Single-stream Policy Optimization (SPO), which eliminates these issues by design.\nL17: SPO replaces per-group baselines with a persistent, KL-adaptive value tracker and normalizes advantages globally across the batch, providing a stable, low-variance learning signal for every sample. Being group-free, SPO enables higher throughput and scales effectively in long-horizon or tool-integrated settings where generation times vary. Furthermore, the persistent value tracker naturally enables an adaptive curriculum via prioritized sampling.\nL18: Experiments using Qwen3-8B show that SPO converges more smoothly and attains higher accuracy than GRPO, while eliminating computation wasted on degenerate groups. Ablation studies confirm that SPO's gains stem from its principled approach to baseline estimation and advantage normalization, offering a more robust and efficient path for LLM reasoning.\nL19: Across five hard math benchmarks with Qwen3 8B, SPO improves the average maj@32 by +3.4 percentage points (pp) over GRPO, driven by substantial absolute point gains on challenging datasets, including +7.3 pp on BRUMO 25, +4.4 pp on AIME 25, +3.3 pp on HMMT 25, and achieves consistent relative gain in pass@$k$ across the evaluated $k$ values.\nL20: SPO's success challenges the prevailing trend of adding incidental complexity to RL algorithms, highlighting a path where fundamental principles, not architectural workarounds, drive the next wave of progress in LLM reasoning.\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2509.13232 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2509.13232v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.13232†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Zhongwen Xu [cite13†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 16 Sep 2025 16:39:11 UTC (230 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Tue, 23 Sep 2025 14:19:47 UTC (262 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Single-stream Policy Optimization, by Zhongwen Xu and Zihan Ding\nL36: \nL37:   * cite8†View PDF L38:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite14†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L47: \nL48: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-09 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite21†cs L53: cite22†cs.AI L54: cite23†stat L55: cite24†stat.ML L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: \nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL76: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL81: \nL82: [In